If the `operator >>` does not return a reference to `istream`, the second input operation (`>> v2`) would not be able to access the `cin` stream, resulting in a compilation error. By returning a reference to `istream`, you allow the chaining of input operations, which is a common and convenient practice in C++. This is why it is recommended to return `istream&` when overloading the input operator.

