samples.guided_completion:
std::iterators are not required to be random access iterators, so you can't just use a temporary variable. However, std::iterators are required to be at least input iterators, which means you can dereference them to get the value pointed to. So you can use a temporary variable of the correct type:

template <typename ITERATOR> void Swap (ITERATOR a, ITERATOR b) {
    typename std::iterator<std::input_iterator_traits<ITERATOR> ::value_type>::type temp = *a;
    *a = *b;
    *b = temp;
}

This will work as long as the type pointed to by the iterator is swappable. If it's not, you can't swap the values pointed to by the iterators, no matter what.

samples.general_completion:
The std::swap function template in the C++ Standard Library already provides this functionality. It uses a technique called "type erasure" to work with any type, including those that are not copyable or assignable. Here is a simplified version of how it could be implemented:

```cpp
template <typename T>
struct Swapper {
    T temp;
    Swapper(T&& t) : temp(std::move(t)) {}
    void operator()(T& a, T& b) const {
        T tmp(std::move(a));
        a = std::move(b);
        b = std::move(tmp);
    }
};

template <typename ITERATOR>
void Swap(ITERATOR a, ITERATOR b) {
    using value_type = typename std::iterator_traits<ITERATOR>::value_type;
    Swapper<value_type> swapper(std::forward<value_type>(*a));
    swapper(*a, *b);
}
```

