memory efficient way of passing large objects in R


I have a function that needs to access a variable in its parent environment (scope from which the function is called). The variable is large in terms of memory so I would prefer not to pass it to by value to the function being called. Is there a standard way of doing this other than declaring the variable in the global scope? For example:

g <- function (a, b) { #do stuff}

f <- function(x) {
    y <- 3 #but in my program y is very large
    g(x, y)
}

I would like to access y in g(). So something like this:

g <- function (a) { a+y }

f <- function(x) {
    y <- 3 #but in my program y is very large
    g(x)
}

Is this possible? Thanks


There is no advantage to "declaring the variable in the global scope" and it may not even be possible in R depending on what you mean by that. You certainly could use the second form. The action that causes duplicate or even triplicate copies of an object is assignment.

samples.guided_completion:
If you don't assign the object to a new name, it won't be copied. So, in your example, y is not copied when you call g(x). It is only copied if you assign it to a new name inside g. If you want to avoid copying, you can use the following form:

g <- function(a) { a + parent.frame()$y }

f <- function(x) {
    y <- 3 #but in my program y is very large
    g(x)
}

This will access the value of y in the environment where f was called from (the parent frame). This is equivalent to the second form you posted, but it makes it explicit that you are accessing a variable in the parent environment.

samples.general_completion:
If you want to avoid copying the object, you can use a reference class or a package like R.oo that provides reference semantics. However, these solutions are not as straightforward as the simple approach of not assigning the object within the function. If you do not assign the object within the function, R will not make a copy of it. Here is an example:

```R
f <- function(x) {
    y <- 3 # but in my program y is very large
    g <- function(a) { a + y }
    g(x)
}

# Now you can call f without worrying about copying y
```

In this example, y is not assigned within g, so R does not make a copy of it. This is a memory-efficient way to pass large objects in R. Note that this approach relies on the lexical scoping rules of R, which means that functions remember the environment in which they were created, and can access variables from that environment. This is a powerful feature of R that can be used to avoid unnecessary copying of large objects.

