The above code works, but it is not very efficient. It computes the sum of all the values in the list, even though it only needs to compute the sum of the values of the items that are returned. We can make this more efficient by using a different approach.

We can use the `Aggregate` method to compute the sum of the values of the items as we iterate through the list. Then, we can use a `while` loop to continue adding items to our result as long as the sum is less than or equal to our desired value. Here's how you can do it:

```csharp
int sum = 0;
int desiredSum = 5;
List<MyObj> result = new List<MyObj>();

foreach (var item in MyObjList)
{
    sum += item.MyValue;
    if (sum > desiredSum)
        break;
    result.Add(item);
}

// Now result contains the first few items whose sum of MyValue is <= 5
```

